## Phase 3 — OPEC+ and policy reaction function
- Maintain a meeting log (config): date, expectation going in (manual input, with
  source), decision, and classification of outcome vs expectation (bigger, in line,
  smaller, surprise direction).
- Event study: Brent outright, M1–M7 spread, Brent–Dubai and vol at t+1, t+5, t+20,
  by outcome-vs-expectation bucket. Report hit rate, mean, median, payoff ratio and N.
- Pre-meeting positioning: did curve structure, CFTC/ICE positioning and implied vol
  move ahead of the meeting? Is positioning predictive of the reaction?
- Extend the same framework to SPR announcements, sanctions events and major
  geopolitical events (manual event log with source and timestamp).

## Phase 4 — Fundamentals and physical tightness
- Tightness dashboard: inventories vs 5-year range and in days of demand; time spreads
  vs inventory (fitted relationship with residuals as a rich/cheap signal); Brent–WTI
  vs Cushing and export economics; refinery utilisation and margins.
- Global balance sheet: supply (OPEC+ by member, US shale, Brazil, Guyana, Canada,
  other non-OPEC), demand by region and product, refinery runs, implied stock change.
  Most inputs are manual from IEA, EIA STEO and OPEC MOMR: build input templates with
  source, publication date and vintage columns, and validate on load.
- Scenario-driven: e.g. "OPEC+ unwinds 500 kb/d", "China demand −300 kb/d",
  "US shale +200 kb/d". Output implied balance and stock path changes.
- Product balances: regional cracks and stocks (US Gulf, NWE, Singapore), with refinery
  outages and seasonal patterns (driving season, heating season, turnarounds).
- Confirmation table: do fundamentals and flows agree with the macro signal? Flag
  disagreements rather than averaging them away.

## Phase 5 — Oil as a macro indicator
- Growth nowcast: test whether diesel/gasoil cracks and freight lead or confirm PMIs,
  industrial production and economic surprise indices, by region. Rolling correlations,
  lead-lag analysis and out-of-sample tests.
- Consumer signal: gasoline cracks and implied demand vs consumer data.
- Daily shock classifier: label each day by sign of US 10y change and SPX change,
  split into real yield vs breakeven driven, with an oil overlay. Report how crude,
  spreads and cracks behave per regime.
- Regime tags: inflation regime, Fed cycle, dollar trend, VIX and MOVE percentile,
  curve structure (backwardation vs contango), OPEC+ policy stance (manual config).
  Condition all results on regime.

## Phase 6 — Oil's transmission to the rest of macro
- Oil to inflation pricing: sensitivity of 1y inflation swaps and 2y breakevens to
  Brent and gasoline moves; how that sensitivity varies by inflation regime.
- Oil to central bank pricing: does oil shift front-end rates and meeting-dated
  pricing, and when? (e.g. only when inflation is above target)
- Terms-of-trade FX: rolling betas of CAD, NOK, MXN, COP (exporters) and JPY, INR,
  KRW, EUR (importers) to oil, split by supply-driven vs demand-driven oil moves
  (proxy: oil and equities moving in opposite vs same direction). Include an
  exporter-minus-importer FX basket.
- Equity bridge: energy sector vs SPX, airlines vs energy, refiner equities vs cracks
  (do equity investors validate the margin move?), HY energy credit spreads vs crude
  and time spreads.
- Credit and micro: shale and integrated producer scorecard via Bloomberg fundamental
  fields (look up): net debt/EBITDA, interest cover, capex and guidance, free cash
  flow, hedge book coverage where disclosed. Estimate which producers cut activity
  first under price scenarios using breakeven costs (manual input).

## Phase 7 — Volatility
1. Surface monitor: Brent and WTI ATM term structure, risk reversals and butterflies;
   percentiles vs history and heatmaps of changes.
2. Skew regimes: identify periods when skew flips to calls (geopolitical supply risk)
   vs puts (demand fear); study what follows for spot, spreads and realised vol.
3. Variance risk premium: implied minus subsequently realised, by tenor and regime,
   with correctly lagged windows.
4. Event vol: implied vs realised moves around OPEC+ meetings, EIA weekly reports,
   CPI and FOMC.
5. Cross-asset vol RV: OVX vs VIX, crude vol vs USDCAD and USDNOK vol, crude vol vs
   MOVE. Rolling regressions with residual z-scores as rich/cheap signals.
6. Vol vs curve structure: relationship between implied vol and backwardation depth.
7. Flag data gaps explicitly (products vol history may be sparse); never interpolate
   silently.

## Phase 8 — Risk module
- Positions in SQL: outright futures, calendar spreads, cracks, inter-commodity spreads,
  and options (Black-76 on futures).
- Risk: delta by contract month and in spread terms, gamma, vega by expiry, theta.
- Daily P&L attribution: outright, curve shape (PCA on the futures curve: level, slope,
  curvature), cracks, vol, theta, unexplained residual (flag large residuals).
- Stress grid: spot ±5/10/20% × vol ±5/10 points, plus named scenarios, e.g.
  "Hormuz disruption: Brent +25%, backwardation +$5 M1–M7, call skew +5",
  "demand recession: Brent −20%, contango, diesel cracks −30%",
  "Gulf hurricane: products +15%, crude −3%", "OPEC+ surprise unwind".
- Synthetic example book so the module can be demonstrated end to end.

## Phase 9 — Outputs
- Morning dashboard (single HTML page): overnight moves, curve and spread changes,
  cracks, tightness signals, positioning extremes, vol percentiles and skew, macro
  transmission (breakevens, FX), today's events with consensus and implied event move,
  and risk summary.
- Cards per market (Brent, WTI, gasoline, diesel/gasoil, jet): drivers, event
  reactions, regimes, fundamentals and flows, vol profile, warnings.
- Playbook query: given a scenario such as
  `{"brent": "up", "spx": "down", "brent_m1m7": "widening", "call_skew": "up"}`
  or a regime filter, rank oil spreads, cracks, FX, inflation and vol trades by
  historical conditional response, with hit rate, N and a confidence flag.

## Engineering requirements
- Type hints, docstrings, logging; follow repo conventions found in Phase 0.
- No lookahead: rolling estimates lagged; fundamentals, positioning and reports used
  only from publication date, using the vintage available at the time.
- Unit tests with synthetic data (no Bloomberg needed). Test Black-76 pricing and
  greeks against known values; test spread and crack construction (units: $/bbl vs
  cents/gal vs $/tonne conversions).
- Never hardcode credentials. Never modify the existing Bloomberg library; wrap it.
- Keep analytics separate from data access.
- After each phase, summarise what was built, assumptions made, and what I should
  verify before moving on.